WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.620
Professor Dave again, let’s talk about midsegments.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:15.100
We just looked at all kinds of bisectors and
other lines that pertain to triangles, so

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.410
let’s learn just one more type.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:23.240
A midsegment of a triangle is a line segment
that joins the midpoints of two sides of the

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.420
triangle.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:31.230
Every triangle will therefore have precisely
three midsegments, and these three line segments

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.500
form another triangle, the midsegment triangle.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:41.319
Since the vertices of the midsegment triangle
are the midpoints of these sides, we can know

00:00:41.319 --> 00:00:46.050
that the segments on either side of a midpoint
are equal to one another.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:51.389
This is signified by these tick marks, one
line here and one line here means these are

00:00:51.389 --> 00:00:52.750
the same.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.489
Two lines here and two lines here means these
are the same as each other, but not necessarily

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:02.359
the same as the segments with one tick mark.

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:04.729
Three tick marks, same idea.

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:10.920
Furthermore, the triangle midsegment theorem
states that any midsegment of a triangle is

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:17.040
parallel to one of the sides of the triangle
and is also precisely half the length of that

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.040
side.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.740
So here we can see one midsegment and the
side of the triangle that it is parallel to.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:29.840
The midsegment is half the length of the side,
and since the side is split up into two equal

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.310
sections, the midsegment is equal to both
of these two sections.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:40.630
We can say the same thing for the other two
midsegments and their corresponding parallel

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.640
sides.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.329
This allows us to draw all kinds of figures
like this one, where some lengths are given

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:50.100
and others can be determined.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:55.340
If we know the length of this midsegment,
we know this side length.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:00.649
If we know this other side length, then we
know the length of this other midsegment.

00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:05.740
There’s plenty of algebra we can do with
this theorem, so let’s check comprehension.

